---
import { computeFeed, peakText, COLD_F, HOT_F } from '../lib/starter.js';
import { landing } from '../data/landing.ts';
import { href } from '../lib/url.js';

const ratios = ['1:1:1', '1:2:2', '1:5:5', '1:10:10'];
const rows = ratios.map((ratio) => {
  const r = computeFeed({ mode: 'keep', amount: 50, ratio, hydration: 100, mix: { wheat: 0, rye: 0 } });
  const at = (f) => peakText(r.dilution, f, { bare: true });
  return { ratio, flour: r.flour.total, water: r.water, total: r.total, p68: at(68), p75: at(75), p82: at(82) };
});
const ex = computeFeed({ mode: 'total', amount: 110, ratio: '1:5:5', hydration: 100, mix: { wheat: 0, rye: 0 } });
const pk = (f) => peakText(ex.dilution, f, { bare: true });
const glance = [20, 50, 100, 200].map((amount) => {
  const g = computeFeed({ mode: 'keep', amount, ratio: '1:5:5', hydration: 100, mix: { wheat: 0, rye: 0 } });
  return { amount, flour: g.flour.total, water: g.water, total: g.total };
});
---
<section aria-labelledby="about-tool" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pt-16">
  <article class="prose-site">
    <h2 id="about-tool" style="margin-top:0">What a sourdough starter feeding calculator does</h2>
    <p>A sourdough starter feeding calculator turns a feeding ratio into grams. You say how much starter you want to keep, or how much you need in total. You pick a ratio such as 1:5:5. The calculator tells you how much starter, flour and water to use. It also gives a rough guess of when the starter will peak in your kitchen.</p>

    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>Starter I keep</strong> if you know how much starter stays in the jar. Choose <strong>Total I need</strong> if a recipe asks for a set weight of fed starter.</li>
      <li>Type the amount in grams, or tap a quick amount.</li>
      <li>Pick a ratio. Use 1:1:1 for a fast feeding. Use 1:5:5 or 1:10:10 for a slower rise. Pick Custom for any other mix.</li>
      <li>Change the hydration if your starter is not at 100%. Enter your kitchen temperature to see the peak time guide.</li>
      <li>Weigh the amounts on a kitchen scale. Use Copy amounts to paste them into a note.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="ratio-meaning">What the feeding ratio numbers mean</h2>
    <p>A ratio of 1:5:5 means 1 part starter, 5 parts flour and 5 parts water, all by weight. If you keep 50 g of starter, you add 250 g of flour and 250 g of water. The jar then holds 550 g. A bigger feeding spreads the yeast and bacteria thinner, so the starter takes longer to peak. That gives a slower rise and a milder flavor.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption class="sr-only">Common feeding ratios for 50 g of starter, with rough peak times at three kitchen temperatures</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Ratio</th><th scope="col">Add to 50 g starter</th><th scope="col">Peak at 68 &deg;F</th><th scope="col">Peak at 75 &deg;F</th><th scope="col">Peak at 82 &deg;F</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => <tr><th scope="row">{r.ratio}</th><td>{r.flour} g flour, {r.water} g water</td><td>{r.p68}</td><td>{r.p75}</td><td>{r.p82}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The times assume an active starter at 100% hydration fed with white flour. Treat them as a guide.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1:1:1</strong> is fast. Use it to wake a starter from the fridge or when you want to bake today.</li>
      <li><strong>1:2:2</strong> is a steady middle path for a starter you feed twice a day.</li>
      <li><strong>1:5:5</strong> suits one feeding a day on the counter.</li>
      <li><strong>1:10:10</strong> suits an overnight feeding, or a warm kitchen where a smaller feed would peak too early.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How much to feed 20 g, 50 g, 100 g or 200 g of starter</h2>
    <p>These amounts use a 1:5:5 feeding at 100% hydration. Keep the starter amount in the jar, then add the flour and water.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption class="sr-only">Flour and water to add to common starter amounts at 1:5:5</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Starter you keep</th><th scope="col">Flour</th><th scope="col">Water</th><th scope="col">Total</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {glance.map((g) => <tr><th scope="row">{g.amount} g</th><td>{g.flour} g</td><td>{g.water} g</td><td>{g.total} g</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>A bread recipe asks for 110 g of ripe starter. You feed at 1:5:5 and want exactly that amount. Choose <strong>Total I need</strong>, enter 110 and pick 1:5:5. The calculator gives {ex.starter} g of starter, {ex.flour.total} g of flour and {ex.water} g of water. Mix them in a clean jar and mark the level with a rubber band. In a 75 &deg;F kitchen, expect the peak in {pk(75)}. If your jar holds more starter than {ex.starter} g, discard the extra first.</p>

    <h2>How temperature changes the timing</h2>
    <p>Yeast works faster when it is warm. As a rule of thumb, the wait gets about half as long for every 18 &deg;F (10 &deg;C) of extra warmth. The same 1:5:5 feeding takes {pk(68)} at 68 &deg;F and {pk(82)} at 82 &deg;F. Below {COLD_F} &deg;F the starter slows a lot. Above {HOT_F} &deg;F it can turn sour and weak.</p>
    <p>If your kitchen is cold, try the oven with only the light on, or the top of the fridge. Do not rely on the clock alone. A ripe starter has a domed top, many bubbles and a level that has about doubled. Feed it at or just after the peak.</p>

    <h2>Hydration and flour mix</h2>
    <p>Most bakers keep starter at 100% hydration, which means equal weights of flour and water. A stiff starter at 60% to 80% uses less water. When you change the hydration here, the total weight of the feeding stays the same and the split between flour and water changes. The calculator assumes your starter already has that hydration.</p>
    <p>Whole wheat and rye give the yeast more to eat. A starter fed with them often peaks sooner and may not rise as tall. Open Flour mix to split the flour weight, for example 80% white and 20% whole wheat.</p>

    <h2>How much to discard</h2>
    <p>Type the weight of the starter in your jar into <strong>Starter in my jar now</strong>. The Discard card then says how much to remove, and how much flour and water to add. If the jar holds less than the amount you want to keep, the card says how many grams are missing.</p>

    <h2>Feeding guides for specific cases</h2>
    <ul>
      {landing.map((l) => <li><a href={href(`${l.slug}/`)}>{l.linkText}</a></li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Tips for steady feeding</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Weigh, do not scoop. Grams are the same every time. Cups are not.</li>
      <li>Use the same jar. Mark the starting level with a band or tape so you can see the rise.</li>
      <li>Feed at the same time each day. A steady schedule gives a steady starter.</li>
      <li>Keep a starter in the fridge if you bake less than once a week. Feed it about weekly. Before baking, feed it once or twice at room temperature.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</section>
